.TH CHMAP_REMOVE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_remove \- remove a key-value pair (type-inferred)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

ccol_retval_t chmap_remove(hm_name, key);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Type-inferred wrapper for chmap_delete_elem() that returns the result
code, so a key_not_found does not stop the program.

The lookup key is converted through the DECLARED key type of the map,
the same way chmap_insert(3) converts it, so a key stored through that
conversion is only found again by a lookup that makes the same
conversion. A literal and a computed expression both work.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  Hash map to remove from
key      Key to remove
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success when the key was removed
ccol_key_not_found when the map does not hold the key
ccol_invalid_args when key is a NULL character pointer, since that is
not a string
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Does not stop the program for key_not_found
Frees the memory of the key and of the value
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
int key = 42;
ccol_retval_t r = chmap_remove(map, key);
if (r == ccol_key_not_found) {
  printf("Key not found\en");
}
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_delete_elem (3),
.BR chmap_insert (3)
